{"purpose":"Record the defect that the universal capture gateway emits drafts which are structurally unable to pass conformance, making a headline v1.10 capability functionally unusable, and state the acceptance criteria for fixing it.","content":"## 1. Background\nADR-035 defines the universal capture gateway as the single deterministic entry point for turning repository activity into draft knowledge. `eka capture --reconcile --from-git` is presented in the eka-cli README as a shipped v1.10 capability that scaffolds drafts with `provenance=reconciled`, `sourceCommitSha`, and a confidence score.\n\n## 2. Observed behaviour\nThe gateway produces drafts that cannot be published. Every reconciled draft inspected in the live workspace under `~/.eka/drafts/eka/` carries no `dimension` field:\n\n```\nbug  fix-reconciled-changes-anvil-a2a6   dim=None\nbug  fix-reconciled-changes-anvil        dim=None\nbug  fix-reconciled-changes-get          dim=None\nspec spec-reconciled-spec-anvil          dim=None\nspec spec-reconciled-spec-content        dim=None\n```\n\n`eka draft validate spec:spec-reconciled-spec-anvil` refuses with a blocking error:\n\n```\n[error] R6 ...: missing `dimension` on knowledge artifact type \"spec\"\nVerdict: FAIL\n```\n\nBecause `eka publish` validates at CKO level and R6 is blocking, none of these drafts can ever be published. The gateway therefore produces a permanent backlog of unpublishable files rather than knowledge.\n\n## 3. Secondary defect — transport directories are classified\n`classifyGitHeuristic` judges the artifact type by substring-matching the joined file list. That list includes snapshot transport paths such as `exchange/snapshots/units/eka/dec-parallel-container-execution-v1/unit.json`, so the token `spec` or `plan` appearing in a *snapshot filename* decides the type of the captured draft. The identical class of defect was already fixed for the code graph in `eka-core/codegraph` (transport directories skipped) but was never applied to capture, so capture re-acquires the noise the code graph deliberately excludes.\n\n## 4. Tertiary defect — contradictory confidence branch\nIn `classifyGitHeuristic` the below-threshold branch assigns `confidence = threshold - 0.05`, which is by construction still below the threshold the branch is meant to satisfy, then clamps to a floor of 0.4. The comment claims the draft is \"still create[d] but flagged low\", a state the arithmetic cannot produce.\n\n## 5. Root cause\nThe git-reconcile classifier lives only in `eka-cli/cmd/capture.go`. `eka-core/runtime/capture.go` exposes `CaptureService.Evaluate`, `ContainsVerb`, and dedupe helpers, but the git path calls the helpers directly and never routes type-and-dimension selection through the core service. The classifier is thus a CLI-local copy with no conformance obligation, while ADR-035 places the gateway in eka-core.\n\n## 6. Acceptance criteria\n- A reconciled draft carries a valid `dimension` for every knowledge-artifact type, so `eka draft validate` passes cleanly for captured drafts.\n- Transport directories (`exchange/`, `.eka/`, drafts, feedback) are excluded from the file list the classifier inspects.\n- Type selection no longer depends on snapshot filenames; a change confined to Go sources under `cmd/` never yields a knowledge-artifact token such as `spec-` or `adr-`.\n- The below-threshold confidence branch yields a value consistent with its stated intent, or is removed if no such state is wanted.\n- Classification and dimension assignment live in eka-core (single implementation), with eka-cli delegating.\n- A regression test asserts that a captured draft for each supported token validates without a blocking finding."}